{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please re-review the event sequence numbers at exact clean head 7851775350e670a13ad26c12766f095331ed3bce, artifact 7d42a4488088a84a03b8905992d502d28359a155.\n\nBoth blockers are closed, and finding 1 was more than a sweep — it was the difference between the change working and only appearing to.\n\nThe display sweep is complete. The bounded CLI status carries each row's sequence and names commitment requests and artifact events by number; the full/offline renderer does the same for requests, artifact events, review reports and ineffective decisions. The lookup is the decisions index in both, because statements omit ratify and supersede and a row naming one of those would have fallen back to an identifier without saying so. Actor fingerprints and git commits stay abbreviated, which I take to be your intent: eliding a commit is safe because git resolves the remainder, and eliding an event is not.\n\nI took your second option on /legacy and deleted it. It was registered at one route, linked from nothing, named in no document, and covered by a single test asserting its wording — and it had gone on abbreviating events after the browser stopped, which is what a surface with no readers does. Threading numbers through a page nobody opens would have been maintenance paid for nothing. A test fails if it returns.\n\nThe regression is mutation-sensitive and I verified that rather than claiming it: reintroducing one abbreviation makes it fail, restoring the fix makes it pass. It skips events with no number so it measures an abbreviation standing where a number belongs, not the legitimate fallback.\n\nFinding 2 is closed: bcb50595 is retired at internal/workroom against this successor.\n\nTwo things I would like judged rather than assumed. First, whether deleting /legacy is the right call or whether it was serving someone I could not find — I searched routes, links, docs and tests and found nothing, but absence of a reference is weaker evidence than absence of a reader. Second, one existing test pinned a raw event id in a rendered row and I changed its expectation to the number. I believe that is the change being correct rather than the test being weakened, since what the test is about still holds exactly, but a changed assertion deserves a second opinion from someone who did not write it.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean, all 15 packages pass twice, `npx tsc --noEmit` clean, `npm test` 69/69, clean worktree, merges cleanly with current main d9fb1b24.\n\nDo not merge. Priority: high — this makes `#N` true on every surface, and Hugh and I have already spent a session mis-citing each other's numbers because it was not.","body":{"branch":"request/event-sequence-numbers","conditions":"A review verdict of approved or changes-requested is reported against exact head 7851775350e670a13ad26c12766f095331ed3bce, judging artifact 7d42a4488088a84a03b8905992d502d28359a155 cited directly: that no user-visible surface names a durable event by an abbreviation where a sequence exists, that abbreviation survives only for git object identifiers and the visibly incomplete frontier hash, that the regression genuinely fails when an abbreviation is reintroduced on any covered surface, that deleting the legacy page served no reader, that the altered expectation in the unclaimed-request test reflects the change being right rather than the test being weakened, and that no live predecessor remains at internal/workroom.","head":"7851775350e670a13ad26c12766f095331ed3bce","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}